#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082808 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "54852305-01f3-5036-a31c-d7ddcfd7c100" :question/math-academy-id "e-10717" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{u^2} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{a}-\\frac{1}{1}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082918 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"}] :question/id #uuid "14b4c34b-bbb0-5a1a-b987-b2a60f0e9fc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-511" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_2^\\infty \\dfrac { 8}{t^5}  \\,\\textrm{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{t}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{-4}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-2(0-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082919 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit"}] :question/id #uuid "efc8fbd2-c814-5c0b-b781-81829ffda2e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-512" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that, this time, it's the *lower* limit of integration that's unbounded.  \n\nFirst, we substitute $u=x-4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty$ when $x\\to -\\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-4$ | $-\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\\int_{-\\infty}^{-4} \\dfrac{1}{u^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082920 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "714c93f9-6170-52a9-849b-2e9d94805c4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-513" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x} }\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{-2} \\frac{1}{(1+x)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\,\\textrm{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\n- For the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[2{x}^{1/2}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[\\sqrt{x}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\sqrt{b}-1] \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\n- For the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{1+x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[-\\frac{1}{1-2}+\\frac{1}{1+a}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{1+a}+1] \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\n- For the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\nIn conclusion, only integral I is divergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71522e6f-7b00-5d83-830e-5e5de40bdf73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288818} {:answer/id #uuid "cbaec9cb-c599-5ba8-ace7-af27043df259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288819} {:answer/id #uuid "092eebfb-3ba9-5e64-96e1-5946b9895a40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288820} {:answer/id #uuid "e46d703b-f925-5221-bda1-c2baf909091a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288821} {:answer/id #uuid "63261215-1e5f-5294-8542-0b1c08bb9fef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e46d703b-f925-5221-bda1-c2baf909091a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288821} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1fe9e7b-b0b1-5b9c-b4be-e4a5d4296639" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7047774a-5515-50b6-ae41-3214d72cda4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-101735" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z$\n- ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2(-2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{z+3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a+3}+\\frac{1}{0+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals III is divergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc0076a4-053c-5d0e-a5de-f00509b76469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288860} {:answer/id #uuid "1540e31f-8803-5442-bb93-2f0aac2a50e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288861} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf0e9a5-84b0-5ab4-8c5b-11285ec9c003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288862} {:answer/id #uuid "50b56624-6b25-5280-bae4-4c5cab999c6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288863} {:answer/id #uuid "0a330799-bcaa-52e3-96f3-e8a04f4b2567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1540e31f-8803-5442-bb93-2f0aac2a50e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288861} :answer-field/id #uuid "1090056a-5ddf-55ac-a3bc-d49ea2dde0ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "729d01e4-2732-59fe-897c-1e548a2a6adf" :question/math-academy-id "q-152762" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{8}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{8}^{a}{z}^{-4/3}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-3{z}^{-1/3}]}_{8}^{a} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{z}}]}_{8}^{a} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{a}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{8}}) \\\\  & =-6(0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2401ff7-53d7-5fc9-93dc-af89ac1a8b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2401ff7-53d7-5fc9-93dc-af89ac1a8b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288877} :answer-field/id #uuid "96c69c26-abf4-5aec-b4bf-24a816db0a50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8660f5f1-f7ea-5da0-ac60-28e25b93ef09" :question/math-academy-id "q-350532" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(5\\theta +1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=5\\theta +1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $\\theta \\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=5\\theta +1$ is as follows:\n\n| $\\theta $ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(5\\theta +1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(5\\theta +1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{5}{u}^{-2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c61687b0-9008-5ef3-a7d4-e1635208a08b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c61687b0-9008-5ef3-a7d4-e1635208a08b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288880} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4fe2876-2127-51de-8836-dd27c832933e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ffe81441-8aee-5b69-8444-b663eb3c8065" :question/math-academy-id "q-350534" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{4}+2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}+2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-1/2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{1/2}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{1/2}}] \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86525533-bd5b-5065-89f0-8788651ce48a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081831} {:answer/id #uuid "996531bc-edb5-5093-b4b8-1db7bbe69f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081832} {:answer/id #uuid "21b23561-bed6-51fb-b64f-574e18912063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081833} {:answer/id #uuid "536796e4-8bd5-5fea-9a7f-68e198cabaca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081834} {:answer/id #uuid "5eca8ddf-a39b-592f-b35e-9899f82240eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "536796e4-8bd5-5fea-9a7f-68e198cabaca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081834} :answer-field/id #uuid "9038bffe-853f-51eb-a75b-c72d158db212" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "998df1b1-461f-5fc4-87ac-c188c53c68e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-6032" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- ${\\int }_{10}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{10}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{10}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{x-1}]}_{10}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-2\\sqrt{10-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-6) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|) \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a}+\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals I and II are divergent."}]}
